{"name":"Fantasia","hex":"#73788b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Fantasia","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A surname from Italian."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To modify a player character's appearance via a Fantasia potion."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Fantasia"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fantasia (1940 film)","blocks":[{"text":"Fantasia is a 1940 American animated anthology film produced by Walt Disney Productions. The third Disney animated feature film, it consists of eight animated segments set to pieces of classical music conducted by Leopold Stokowski, seven of which are performed by the Philadelphia Orchestra. Music critic and composer Deems Taylor acts as the film's Master of Ceremonies who introduces each segment in live-action. The film's production was supervised by Walt Disney and Ben Sharpsteen, with story direction by Joe Grant and Dick Huemer."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/6/6b/Fantasia1940.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fantasia_(1940_film)"},{"title":"Fantasia 2000","blocks":[{"text":"Fantasia 2000 is a 1999 American animated musical anthology film produced by Walt Disney Feature Animation and the sequel to Fantasia (1940). Like its predecessor, the film consists of animated segments set to pieces of classical music. Segments are introduced by Steve Martin, Itzhak Perlman, Quincy Jones, Bette Midler, James Earl Jones, Penn & Teller, James Levine, and Angela Lansbury in live action scenes directed by Don Hahn, while the animated sequences are directed by Pixote Hunt, Hendel Butoy, Eric Goldberg, James Algar, Francis Glebas and Paul and Gaëtan Brizzi."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/c/c5/Fantasia2000_Poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fantasia_2000"},{"title":"Fantasia (singer)","blocks":[{"text":"Fantasia Monique Barrino-Taylor, also known mononymously as Fantasia, is an American singer and actress. She rose to prominence in 2004 with her performance of the Porgy and Bess standard \"Summertime\" during the third season of American Idol, becoming that season's winner. Following her victory, Barrino became the second artist after Lauryn Hill to have their first commercial single debut atop the Billboard Hot 100 with her 2004 song, \"I Believe\". It went on to become the best-selling single in the United States that year."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Fantasia_Barrino.jpg/330px-Fantasia_Barrino.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fantasia_(singer)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Fantasia_theatrical_trailer.png/500px-Fantasia_theatrical_trailer.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Fantasia_theatrical_trailer.png/500px-Fantasia_theatrical_trailer.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Title screen in the original theatrical trailer","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFantasia_theatrical_trailer.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Leopold_Stokowski_LOC_26447u.jpg/500px-Leopold_Stokowski_LOC_26447u.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Leopold_Stokowski_LOC_26447u.jpg/1280px-Leopold_Stokowski_LOC_26447u.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Leopold_Stokowski_LOC_26447u.jpg/500px-Leopold_Stokowski_LOC_26447u.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Leopold Stokowski conducted the film's score.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALeopold_Stokowski_LOC_26447u.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Deems_Taylor_%281885-1966%29.jpg/500px-Deems_Taylor_%281885-1966%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Deems_Taylor_%281885-1966%29.jpg/500px-Deems_Taylor_%281885-1966%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Deems Taylor was the film's Master of Ceremonies, who introduced each segment in live-action interstitial scenes.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADeems_Taylor_(1885-1966).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/5/51/Disney_taylor_stokowski.jpg/330px-Disney_taylor_stokowski.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/5/51/Disney_taylor_stokowski.jpg/330px-Disney_taylor_stokowski.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Disney acting out a scene in The Sorcerer's Apprentice for Taylor and Stokowski","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADisney_taylor_stokowski.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Phila_AcademyofMusicl03.JPG/500px-Phila_AcademyofMusicl03.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Phila_AcademyofMusicl03.JPG/1280px-Phila_AcademyofMusicl03.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Phila_AcademyofMusicl03.JPG/500px-Phila_AcademyofMusicl03.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"With exception to The Sorcerer's Apprentice, the film's score was recorded at the Philadelphia Academy of Music.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APhila_AcademyofMusicl03.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Promises_Promises_at_Broadway_Theatre.JPG/500px-Promises_Promises_at_Broadway_Theatre.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Promises_Promises_at_Broadway_Theatre.JPG/1280px-Promises_Promises_at_Broadway_Theatre.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Promises_Promises_at_Broadway_Theatre.JPG/500px-Promises_Promises_at_Broadway_Theatre.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The film's first roadshow opened at the Broadway Theatre in New York City on November 13, 1940.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APromises_Promises_at_Broadway_Theatre.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/7a/Fantasia_1969_Re-release_poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/7a/Fantasia_1969_Re-release_poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"1969 psychedelic-style re-release poster","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFantasia_1969_Re-release_poster.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Carthay_Circle_Theater.jpg/500px-Carthay_Circle_Theater.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Carthay_Circle_Theater.jpg/1280px-Carthay_Circle_Theater.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Carthay_Circle_Theater.jpg/500px-Carthay_Circle_Theater.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_